                                        | Generate an `AGENTS.md` scaffold in the current directory.      | Capture persistent instructions for the repository or subdirectory you're working in.                      |\n7088:| [`/goal`](#set-or-view-a-task-goal-with-goal)                                               | Set, edit, pause, resume, view, or clear a task goal.           | Give Codex a persistent target to track while a larger task runs.                                          |\n7165:#### Set or view a task goal with `/goal`\n7167:1. Type `/goal ` to set the goal, for example `/goal Finish the migration and keep tests green`.\n7168:2. Type `/goal` to view the current goal.\n7169:3. Use `/goal edit` to revise the objective. Use `/goal pause`, `/goal resume`, or `/goal clear` to pause, resume, or remove it.\n7171:Expected: Codex keeps the goal attached to the active task while work continues.\n7173:Goal objectives must be non-empty and at most 4,000 characters. For longer\n7174:instructions, put the details in a file and point the goal at that file.\n7190:functionality, the built-in browser, file previews, plugins, and skills.\n7397:In the ChatGPT desktop app, worktrees let Codex run multiple independent tasks in the same project without interfering with each other. For Git repositories, [scheduled tasks](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/automations) can run on dedicated background worktrees so they don't conflict with your ongoing work. In non-version-controlled projects, scheduled tasks run directly in the project directory. You can also start tasks in a worktree manually and use Handoff to move a task between Local and Worktree.\n7649:## Customization, Skills, Rules, MCP, and Integrations\n7653:How to shape Codex behavior with instructions, skills, prompts, MCP, and external integrations.\n7731:3. Add bundled skills if ChatGPT should follow repeatable workflows alongside\n7746:workflow, start with a local skill. Build a plugin when you want to share that\n7747:workflow across teams, bundle connectors or MCP config, package lifecycle hooks,\n7750:A plugin can include skills, an MCP-backed app, or both. If your plugin needs\n7761:For the fastest setup, use the built-in `@plugin-creator` skill.\n7810:3. Add any bundled skills under `skills/` if the plugin should include\n7874:Start with a minimal plugin that packages one skill.\n7889:  \"skills\": \"./skills/\"\n7896:2. Add a skill under `skills//SKILL.md`.\n7899:mkdir -p my-first-plugin/skills/hello\n7902:`my-first-plugin/skills/hello/SKILL.md`\n8165:a `skills/` directory, a `hooks/` directory for lifecycle hooks, an `.app.json`\n8170:Only `plugin.json` belongs in `.codex-plugin/`. Keep `skills/`, `hooks/`,\n8177:- Point to bundled components such as skills, connectors, MCP servers, or hooks.\n8187:  \"description\": \"Bundle reusable skills and connectors.\",\n8197:  \"skills\": \"./skills/\",\n8200:  \"hooks\": \"./hooks/hooks.json\",\n8203:    \"shortDescription\": \"Reusable skills and connectors\",\n8204:    \"longDescription\": \"Distribute skills and connectors together.\",\n8226:### Build skills\n8228:Source: [Build skills](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/build-skills.md)\n8230:Use agent skills to extend Codex with task-specific capabilities. A skill packages instructions, resources, and optional scripts so Codex can follow a workflow reliably. Skills build on the [open agent skills standard](https://agentskills.io).\n8232:Skills are the authoring format for reusable workflows. Plugins distribute reusable skills and connectors to ChatGPT Work on the web and to Work and Codex in the ChatGPT desktop app. Codex CLI can also install plugins. Use skills to design the workflow itself, then package it as a [plugin](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/build-plugins) when you want other people in your workspace to install it.\n8234:Skills are available in the ChatGPT desktop app, Codex CLI, and IDE extension.\n8236:In the ChatGPT desktop app, open **Skills** in the sidebar to view and explore skills\n8239:Skills use **progressive disclosure** to manage context efficiently: Codex starts with each skill's name, description, and file path. Codex loads the full `SKILL.md` instructions only when it decides to use a skill.\n8241:Codex includes an initial list of available skills in context so it can choose the right skill for a task. To avoid crowding out the rest of the prompt, this list uses at most 2% of the model’s context window, or 8,000 characters when the context window is unknown. If many skills are installed, Codex shortens skill descriptions first. For large skill sets, Codex may omit some skills from the initial list and show a warning.\n8243:This budget applies only to the initial skills list. When Codex selects a skill, it still reads the full SKILL.md instructions for that skill.\n8245:A skill is a directory with a `SKILL.md` file plus optional scripts and references. The `SKILL.md` file must include `name` and `description`.\n8247:#### How Codex uses skills\n8249:Codex can activate skills in two ways:\n8251:1. **Explicit invocation:** Include the skill directly in your prompt. In CLI/IDE, run `/skills` or type `$` to mention a skill.\n8252:2. **Implicit invocation:** Codex can choose a skill when your task matches the skill `description`.\n8254:Because implicit matching depends on `description`, write concise descriptions with clear scope and boundaries. Front-load the key use case and trigger words so Codex can still match the skill if descriptions are shortened.\n8256:#### Create a skill\n8260:inspects the steps, and drafts a reusable skill from the demonstration.\n8262:If you want to describe the skill instead, use the built-in creator:\n8265:$skill-creator\n8268:The creator asks what the skill does, when it should trigger, and whether it should stay instruction-only or include scripts. Instruction-only is the default.\n8270:You can also create a skill manually by creating a folder with a `SKILL.md` file:\n8274:name: skill-name\n8275:description: Explain exactly when this skill should and should not trigger.\n8278:Skill instructions for Codex to follow.\n8281:Codex detects skill changes automatically. If an update doesn't appear, restart Codex.\n8283:#### Where to save skills\n8285:Codex reads skills from repository, user, admin, and system locations. For repositories, Codex scans `.agents/skills` in every directory from your current working directory up to the repository root. If two skills share the same `name`, Codex doesn't merge them; both can appear in skill selectors.\n8287:| Skill Scope                                                                    | Location                                                                                                                                                                                             | Suggested use                                                                                                                      |\n8289:| `REPO`                                                                         | `$CWD/.agents/skills`                                                                                                                                                                                |\n8290:| Current working directory: where you launch Codex.                             | If you're in a repository or code environment, teams can check in skills relevant to a working folder. For example, skills only relevant to a microservice or a module.                              |\n8291:| `REPO`                                                                         | `$CWD/../.agents/skills`                                                                                                                                                                             |\n8292:| A folder above CWD when you launch Codex inside a Git repository.              | If you're in a repository with nested folders, organizations can check in skills relevant to a shared area in a parent folder.                                                                       |\n8293:| `REPO`                                                                         | `$REPO_ROOT/.agents/skills`                                                                                                                                                                          |\n8294:| The topmost root folder when you launch Codex inside a Git repository.         | If you're in a repository with nested folders, organizations can check in skills relevant to everyone using the repository. These serve as root skills available to any subfolder in the repository. |\n8295:| `USER`                                                                         | `$HOME/.agents/skills`                                                                                                                                                                               |\n8296:| Any skills checked into the user's personal folder.                            | Use to curate skills relevant to a user that apply to any repository the user may work in.                                                                                                           |\n8297:| `ADMIN`                                                                        | `/etc/codex/skills`                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n8298:| Any skills checked into the machine or container in a shared, system location. | Use for SDK scripts, automation, and for checking in default admin skills available to each user on the machine.                                                                                     |\n8299:| `SYSTEM`                                                                       | Bundled with Codex by OpenAI.                                                                                                                                                                        | Useful skills relevant to a broad audience such as the skill-creator and plan skills. Available to everyone when they start Codex. |\n8301:Codex supports symlinked skill folders and follows the symlink target when scanning these locations.\n8304:distribute reusable skills beyond a single repo, or optionally bundle them with\n8307:#### Distribute skills with plugins\n8309:Direct skill folders are best for local authoring and repo-scoped workflows. If\n8310:you want to distribute a reusable skill, bundle two or more skills together, or\n8311:ship a skill alongside a connector, package them as a\n8314:Plugins can include one or more skills. They can also optionally bundle app\n8318:#### Install curated skills for local use\n8320:To add curated skills beyond the built-ins for your own local Codex setup, use `$skill-installer`. For example, to install the `$linear` skill:\n8323:$skill-installer linear\n8326:You can also prompt the installer to download skills from other repositories.\n8327:Codex detects newly installed skills automatically; if one doesn't appear,\n8331:own skills, prefer plugins.\n8333:#### Enable or disable skills\n8335:Use `[[skills.config]]` entries in `~/.codex/config.toml` to disable a skill without deleting it:\n8338:[[skills.config]]\n8339:path = \"/path/to/skill/SKILL.md\"\n8347:Add `agents/openai.yaml` to configure UI metadata in the [ChatGPT desktop app](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/app), to set invocation policy, and to declare tool dependencies for a more seamless experience with using the skill.\n8356:  default_prompt: \"Optional surrounding prompt to use the skill with\"\n8370:`allow_implicit_invocation` (default: `true`): When `false`, Codex won't implicitly invoke the skill based on user prompt; explicit `$skill` invocation still works.\n8374:- Keep each skill focused on one job.\n8377:-