 the project root (typically the Git root), Codex walks down to your current working directory. If Codex cannot find a project root, it only checks the current directory. In each directory along the path, it checks for `AGENTS.override.md`, then `AGENTS.md`, then any fallback names in `project_doc_fallback_filenames`. Codex includes at most one file per directory.\n8650:Create persistent defaults in your Codex home directory so every repository inherits your working agreements.\n8658:2. Create `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` with reusable preferences:\n8661:   # ~/.codex/AGENTS.md\n8676:   Expected: Codex quotes the items from `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` before proposing work.\n8684:1. In your repository root, add an `AGENTS.md` that covers basic setup:\n8687:   # AGENTS.md\n8712:   Expected: Codex reports the global file first, the repository root `AGENTS.md` second, and the payments override last.\n8726:   project_doc_fallback_filenames = [\"TEAM_GUIDE.md\", \".agents.md\"]\n8732:Now Codex checks each directory in this order: `AGENTS.override.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `TEAM_GUIDE.md`, `.agents.md`. Filenames not on this list are ignored for instruction discovery. The larger byte limit allows more combined guidance before truncation.\n8736:Set the `CODEX_HOME` environment variable when you want a different profile, such as a project-specific automation user:\n8761:- Visit the official [AGENTS.md](https://agents.md) website for more information.\n8762:- Review [Prompting Codex](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/prompting) for conversational patterns that pair well with persistent guidance.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f6bda-13d8-77d3-957c-2e50c29f24b7"}}}
