ired entry point. The other manifest\n8224-fields are optional, but published plugins commonly use them.\n8225-\n8226-### Build skills\n--\n8938-- **Client**: the MCP connection inside Codex\n8939-- **Server**: the external tool or context provider\n8940-\n8941:MCP servers can expose:\n8942-\n8943-- **Tools** (actions)\n8944-- **Resources** (readable data)\n--\n8954-\n8955-#### Subagents\n8956-\n8957:You can create different agents with different roles and prompt them to use tools differently. For example, one agent might run specific testing commands and configurations, while another has MCP servers that fetch production logs for debugging. Each subagent stays focused and uses the right tools for its job.\n8958-\n8959-[Subagents](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/subagents)\n8960-\n--\n9004-- inline `[hooks]` tables inside `config.toml`\n9005-\n9006-Installed plugins can also bundle lifecycle config through their plugin\n9007:manifest or a default `hooks/hooks.json` file. See [Build\n9008:plugins](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/build-plugins#bundled-mcp-servers-and-lifecycle-hooks) for the\n9009-plugin packaging rules.\n9010-\n9011-In practice, the four most useful locations are:\n--\n9225-  plugin sources, but still loads managed hooks from `requirements.toml` and\n9226-  other managed config layers.\n9227-\n9228:#### Plugin-bundled hooks\n9229-\n9230-When a plugin is enabled, Codex can load lifecycle hooks from that plugin\n9231-alongside user, project, and managed hooks.\n9232-\n9233:By default, Codex looks for `hooks/hooks.json` inside the plugin root. A plugin\n9234-manifest can override that default with a `hooks` entry in\n9235:`.codex-plugin/plugin.json`. The manifest entry can be a `./`-prefixed path, an\n9236-array of `./`-prefixed paths, an inline hooks object, or an array of inline\n9237-hooks objects.\n9238-\n9239-```json\n9240-{\n9241-  \"name\": \"repo-policy\",\n9242:  \"hooks\": \"./hooks/hooks.json\"\n9243-}\n9244-```\n9245-\n9246