rminal UI (TUI)\n9601-\n9602:In the `codex` TUI, use `/mcp` to see your active MCP servers.\n9603-\n9604-#### Configure in the IDE extension\n9605-\n9606:1. Open the gear menu, then select **MCP servers**.\n9607-2. Select **Add server**.\n9608-3. Enter a name, choose **STDIO** or **Streamable HTTP**, and provide the\n9609-   server's command or URL.\n--\n9715-approval_mode = \"approve\"\n9716-```\n9717-\n9718:#### Plugin-provided MCP servers\n9719-\n9720:Installed plugins can bundle MCP servers in their plugin manifest. Those\n9721-servers are launched from the plugin, so user config doesn't set their\n9722-transport command. User config can still control on/off state and tool policy\n9723-under `plugins..mcp_servers.`.\n--\n9732-approval_mode = \"approve\"\n9733-```\n9734-\n9735:#### Examples of useful MCP servers\n9736-\n9737:The list of MCP servers keeps growing. Here are a few common ones:\n9738-\n9739-- [OpenAI Docs MCP](https://developers.openai.com/learn/docs-mcp): Search and read OpenAI developer docs.\n9740-- [Context7](https://github.com/upstash/context7): Connect to up-to-date developer documentation.\n--\n9812-\n9813-Record & Replay is a fast way to create a skill from a demonstrated workflow.\n9814-If you want to distribute a separate stable package across a team, bundle\n9815:multiple skills, include connectors, add MCP servers, or manage install\n9816-metadata, package that workflow as its own plugin. See\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f6bda-13d8-77d3-957c-2e50c29f24b7"}}}
